 <p>
-<strong>Short answer:</strong> King Khalid International Airport (RUH) in Riyadh has been closed since an attack on the afternoon of Saturday 10 October 2026, and the airport was struck again on Sunday 11 October. On 12 October, GACA announced that the suspension had been extended to complete checks on the safety and operational readiness of the airport's facilities, without giving an end date. A new aviation notice (NOTAM), reported by Reuters on 12 October, keeps the airport closed until 21:00 UTC — midnight Riyadh time — on Saturday 17 October. That is the notice period, not a confirmed reopening: NOTAMs can be extended, and as of this update <strong>no reopening time for passenger flights has been announced by GACA or the other sources we consulted</strong>. Do not go to the airport unless your airline has confirmed your flight is operating.</p>
+<strong>Short answer:</strong> Saudi Arabia's General Authority of Civil Aviation (GACA) suspended operations at King Khalid International Airport (RUH) in Riyadh after an attack on the afternoon of Saturday 10 October 2026; the airport was struck again on Sunday 11 October. On 12 October, GACA extended the suspension to complete checks on the safety and operational readiness of the airport's facilities, without giving an end date. A NOTAM reported by Reuters on 12 October set a closure period ending at 21:00 UTC on Saturday 17 October. Since then, reports have differed: aviation press reported a new notice from 17 October requiring prior GACA permission for all flights, while other outlets reported that the airport remained closed. As of 22 October 2026, <strong>we have not found an official GACA announcement that scheduled passenger flights have resumed</strong>. Do not go to the airport unless your airline has confirmed your flight is operating.</p>
 
