 <h2 id="flights">What This Means for Flights</h2>
-<p>Airlines have cancelled Riyadh flights while the airport is closed. According to The National, Etihad has cancelled flights on Monday 12 and Tuesday 13 October; Emirates cancelled all six Dubai–Riyadh services on Monday and four on Tuesday; flydubai has cancelled Riyadh flights up to and including 14 October; and Air Arabia has cancelled its Sharjah–Riyadh flights on Monday and Tuesday. Other carriers, including EgyptAir, have suspended Riyadh flights until operations resume. Air Data News reported on 12 October that the Lufthansa Group had suspended Riyadh flights through 16 October and Air France-KLM through the end of October — so even after the airport reopens, your airline may not restart its Riyadh service straight away.</p>
+<p>Airlines decide individually when to cancel or restart Riyadh services. In the days after 10 October, several carriers — including Etihad, Emirates, flydubai, Air Arabia and EgyptAir — cancelled or suspended Riyadh flights, according to The National, and Air Data News reported on 12 October that the Lufthansa Group and Air France-KLM had suspended Riyadh flights for longer periods. Later reports said some airlines had pushed their return into November or 2027. Airline plans change frequently, so check your own airline rather than relying on these reports.</p>
 <p>Even after an official reopening, schedules may take time to recover. After the 9 October reopening, hundreds of flights remained delayed or cancelled, and individual airlines decide when to restart their own Riyadh services.</p>
